      en: "The dense upper canopy of the Kakum National Park is a highly distinct, biologically segregated ecosystem hovering up to 40 meters above the dark, damp forest floor. This elevated environment is exposed to significantly more sunlight and intense tropical rains, supporting an entirely different array of flora and fauna than the ground level. It is dominated by massive emergent trees, thick parasitic lianas, and heavy clusters of epiphytic ferns and orchids. Biologically, the canopy is the most active zone of the rainforest, sheltering elusive, arboreal species such as the endangered Diana monkey, flying squirrels, and hundreds of species of specialized frugivorous birds. Sachkunde K3 — rainforest stratification."
